  "answer_text": "The legendary scene where Del Boy falls through the bar occurs in the episode titled **\"Yuppy Love,\"** which is the first episode of **Series 6** of *Only Fools and Horses*. It was first broadcast on the BBC on **January 8, 1989**.\n\nWidely voted as the funniest moment in British television history, the scene is masterfully written and timed. \n\n### The Scene\nDel Boy (David Jason) has embraced the 1980s \"yuppy\" lifestyle and is visiting a trendy London wine bar with Trigger (Roger Lloyd-Pack). Spotting two women across the room, Del decides to make his move. \n\nHe confidently tells Trigger: **\"Play it nice and cool, son, play it nice and cool.\"**\n\nJust as Del turns to lean smoothly against the bar to look sophisticated, the bartender lifts the bar flap to step out. Oblivious, Del leans back into empty space and tumbles straight down onto the floor behind the bar. \n\nWhat elevates the scene from a simple slapstick fall to comedy perfection is Trigger\u2019s delayed, utterly bewildered reaction. He looks around in complete confusion, wondering how Del managed to vanish into thin air in the blink of an eye.\n\n### Behind-the-Scenes Trivia\n* **Trigger wasn't supposed to be there:** A rehearsal script discovered years later revealed that Roger Lloyd-Pack (Trigger) wasn't originally written into this scene. He simply happened to show up on set that day. Writer John Sullivan realized he could use him, quickly writing him into the scene\u2014a decision that resulted in the iconic reaction shot.\n* **Perfect timing:** David Jason and the crew rehearsed the fall extensively to ensure the timing was absolutely perfect. The bartender had to lift the flap at the exact microsecond Del turned his head so the audience would see it, but Del wouldn't. \n* **The \"double take\":** David Jason advised the actor playing the bartender not to look down or react immediately when Del fell, which kept the focus entirely on Trigger's hilarious confusion.",
